Module: SQLBulkImport_New.sql
Filepath: /c:/Users/Levi_/OneDrive - primecaremanagers.com/SQLRoutines/SQL_Import_Update/CCLF_BulkImport/SQLBulkImport_New.sql

Overview:
    - Scans a local folder for CCLF files, parses filenames to derive dates and file types,
        bulk-inserts each file into a staging table, calls a filetype-specific stored procedure
        to process the data, logs success/failure, and finally removes files from the folder.

Prerequisites & Permissions:
    - xp_cmdshell must be enabled and usable by the executing account.
    - SQL Server service account (or proxy used by xp_cmdshell) requires read/write/delete permissions
        on C:\SQLBulkImport\CCLF\Monthly\ and the ability to execute PowerShell.
    - Database objects required:
        - CCLF_Months.dbo.CCLFStaging (staging table for Bulk Insert)
        - CCLF_Procedures.dbo.procBulkImport_<FileType>_2023 (processing procs per filetype)
        - Master.dbo.CCLF_ImportLog (import audit/log table)
        - Database_Constants.dbo.Import_Export_Automated (single-row config updated from filenames)
    - Note: SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER OFF is used at top; verify compatibility with called procs / features.

Inputs / Filename expectations:
    - Files are read from: C:\SQLBulkImport\CCLF\Monthly\
    - PowerShell enumerates file Name and Length => inserted as ""Name|Length"".
    - Each filename must include one of the connector tokens (see Connector mapping) and
        must contain a 6-character date token located within the last 15 characters of the filename.
    - The 6-character token must be castable to DATE by SQL Server in the current language/format
        or the substring/parsing logic must be adapted.

Connector -> FileType mapping (temp1):
    - ZC0Y => CCLF0
    - ZC1Y => CCLF1
    - ZC2Y => CCLF2
    - ZC3Y => CCLF3
    - ZC4Y => CCLF4
    - ZC5Y => CCLF5
    - ZC6Y => CCLF6
    - ZC7Y => CCLF7
    - ZC8Y => CCLF8
    - ZC9Y => CCLF9
    - ZCAY => CCLFA
    - ZCBY => CCLFB

Key variables (high-level):
    - @filename, @filesizeMB  : filename and size (MB) from folder listing
    - @connector, @filetype   : matched connector and mapped filetype
    - @MedicareFileDate, @CCLFFileDate, @CCLFFileYear, @FiscalYear : derived date values from filename
    - @CCLFFileStructure      : constructed as <FileType>_<Year>
    - @CCLFFileLocation       : full filesystem path to file
    - @sql, @ErrorMessage     : dynamic SQL and error capture for logging

Processing flow:
    1. Populate #filelistraw with file lines (Name|Length) via xp_cmdshell + PowerShell.
    2. Parse lines into #filelist (Filename, FilesizeMB).
    3. Abort if no files found.
    4. Derive global constants (FiscalYear, MedicareFileDate, CCLFFileDate, etc.) from filename substrings
         and update Database_Constants.dbo.Import_Export_Automated (id = 1).
    5. Cursor over files joined to #temp1 (matching connector -> filetype):
         a. Clear CCLF_Months.dbo.CCLFStaging.
         b. Bulk insert the file into staging:
                - Bulk insert targets CCLFStaging using ROWTERMINATOR = 0x0A (LF).
         c. Execute filetype-specific proc: CCLF_Procedures.dbo.procBulkImport_<FileType>_2023
                with parameters: MedicareFileDate, CCLFFileDate, FileType, CCLFFileStructure, CCLFFileLocation.
         d. On success, insert a success row into Master.dbo.CCLF_ImportLog.
         e. On error (TRY/CATCH), capture ERROR_MESSAGE() and insert a failure row into the log.
    6. After processing all files, delete all files from the working folder: del /q ""C:\SQLBulkImport\CCLF\Monthly\*""

Audit & Error Handling:
    - Each file processed yields a row in Master.dbo.CCLF_ImportLog with Filename, FileType, FilesizeMB,
        Success flag (1/0), Message (static success or ERROR_MESSAGE()), and DateImported.
    - TRY/CATCH ensures per-file error capture and continued processing for remaining files.

Important Caveats & Recommendations:
    - Relying on xp_cmdshell and PowerShell has security implications. Prefer using SQL Agent job steps
        with CmdExec / PowerShell or a controlled proxy account rather than enabling xp_cmdshell globally.
    - Verify the filename date extraction logic: left(right(filename,15),6) must reliably extract a valid
        date token for all file naming patterns. Consider using a more explicit regex/parse or standardize filenames.
    - Bulk insert options: consider specifying FIELDTERMINATOR, CODEPAGE, FIRSTROW, and ERRORFILE for robust imports.
    - The processing procedure name contains a hard-coded year suffix (_2023). If processing other years,
        change to dynamic proc naming or remove the hard-coded year.
    - Consider replacing the cursor with a set-based operation or a table-driven loop for scalability.
    - Ensure transactional behavior if required (e.g., rollback on proc failure) and consider retention/archive
